#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:43
msgid "Background"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:45
msgid ""
"Where unit tests focus on validating a very specific piece of functionality, "
"integration tests focus on validating the interaction between pieces of "
"code. In ROS 2 this is often accomplished by launching a system of one or "
"several nodes, for example the `Gazebo simulator <https://gazebosim.org/"
"home>`__ and the `Nav2 navigation <https://github.com/ros-planning/"
"navigation2.git>`__ stack. As a result, these tests are more complex both to "
"set up and to run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:49
msgid ""
"A key aspect of ROS 2 integration testing is that nodes that are part of "
"different tests shouldn't communicate with each other, even when run in "
"parallel. This will be achieved here using a specific test runner that picks "
"unique :doc:`ROS domain IDs <../../../../ROS-Framework/nodes/About-Domain-"
"ID>`. In addition, integration tests have to fit in the overall testing "
"workflow. A standardized approach is to ensure each test outputs an XUnit "
"file, which are easily parsed using common test tooling."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:55
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:57
msgid ""
"The main tool in use here is the `launch_testing <https://docs.ros.org/en/"
"rolling/p/launch_testing/index.html>`_ package (`launch_testing repository "
"<https://github.com/ros2/launch/tree/rolling/launch_testing>`_). This ROS-"
"agnostic functionality can extend a Python launch file with both active "
"tests (that run while the nodes are also running) and post-shutdown tests "
"(which run once after all nodes have exited). ``launch_testing`` relies on "
"the Python standard module `unittest <https://docs.python.org/3/library/"
"unittest.html>`_ for the actual testing. To get our integration tests run as "
"part of ``colcon test``, we register the launch file in the ``CMakeLists."
"txt`` or ``setup.py`` file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:63
msgid ""
"For waiting on topics and triggering actions based on publisher "
"availability, the `launch_testing_ros <https://docs.ros.org/en/rolling/p/"
"launch_testing_ros/>`_ package provides the `WaitForTopics <https://docs.ros."
"org/en/rolling/p/launch_testing_ros/launch_testing_ros.wait_for_topics."
"html>`_ utility, which simplifies topic subscription and waiting logic in "
"integration tests."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:66
msgid "Steps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:69
msgid "1 Describe the test in the test launch file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:71
msgid ""
"Both the nodes under test and the tests themselves are launched using a "
"Python launch file, which resembles a ROS 2 Python launch file. It is "
"customary to make the integration test launch file names follow the pattern "
"``test/test_*.py``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:74
msgid ""
"There are two common types of tests in integration testing: active tests, "
"which run while the nodes under test are running, and post-shutdown tests, "
"which are run after exiting the nodes. We will cover both in this tutorial."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:78
msgid "1.1 Imports"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:80
msgid ""
"We first start by importing the Python modules we will be using. Key modules "
"for testing include the general-purpose ``unittest``, ``launch_testing``, "
"and the ``WaitForTopics`` utility from ``launch_testing_ros`` for convenient "
"topic subscription and waiting logic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:83
msgid ""
"import math\n"
"import threading\n"
"import unittest\n"
"from typing import Any\n"
"\n"
"import launch\n"
"import launch_ros.actions\n"
"import launch_testing\n"
"import launch_testing.actions\n"
"import launch_testing.markers\n"
"import pytest\n"
"from geometry_msgs.msg import Twist\n"
"from launch_testing_ros import WaitForTopics\n"
"from launch_testing_ros.actions import EnableRmwIsolation\n"
"from rclpy.node import Node\n"
"from rclpy.publisher import PublisherEventCallbacks\n"
"from turtlesim_msgs.msg import Pose"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:104
msgid "1.2 Generate the test description"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:106
msgid ""
"The function ``generate_test_description`` describes what to launch, similar "
"to ``generate_launch_description`` in a ROS 2 Python launch file. In the "
"example below, we launch the turtlesim node with immediate test execution "
"(no arbitrary delays)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:109
msgid ""
"The ``EnableRmwIsolation`` action ensures that ROS communication is isolated "
"using ``rmw_test_fixture``, preventing test interference. The "
"``ReadyToTest`` action signals the test framework that the tests should "
"begin."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:112
msgid ""
"@pytest.mark.launch_test\n"
"@launch_testing.markers.keep_alive\n"
"def generate_test_description() -> launch.LaunchDescription:\n"
"    \"\"\"Create the launch description for turtlesim integration tests.\n"
"\n"
"    Returns:\n"
"        launch.LaunchDescription: Launch actions that start turtlesim and\n"
"            signal when tests can begin.\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"    return launch.LaunchDescription(\n"
"        [\n"
"            # Action which enables isolation of ROS communication using "
"rmw_test_fixture\n"
"            EnableRmwIsolation(),\n"
"            # Node under test: turtlesim_node\n"
"            launch_ros.actions.Node(\n"
"                package=\"turtlesim\",\n"
"                namespace=\"\",\n"
"                executable=\"turtlesim_node\",\n"
"                name=\"turtle1\",\n"
"                output=\"screen\",\n"
"            ),\n"
"            launch_testing.actions.ReadyToTest(),\n"
"        ]\n"
"    )"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:139
msgid ""
"In more complex integration test setups, you will probably want to launch a "
"system of several nodes, together with additional nodes that perform mocking "
"or must otherwise interact with the nodes under test."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:142
msgid "1.3 Active tests using WaitForTopics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:144
msgid ""
"The active tests interact with the running nodes. The ``WaitForTopics`` "
"utility from ``launch_testing_ros`` provides a convenient way to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:147
msgid "Subscribe to topics and wait for them to become available"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:148
msgid "Receive messages published on those topics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:149
msgid "Optionally trigger actions (like publishing control messages)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:152
msgid "1.3.1 Basic topic subscription test"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:154
msgid ""
"The simplest test verifies that a topic is published and messages are "
"received:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:156
msgid ""
"class TestTurtleSimWithWaitForTopics(unittest.TestCase):\n"
"    def test_publishes_pose_with_wait_for_topics(self) -> None:\n"
"        \"\"\"Verify pose messages are received for turtlesim.\"\"\"\n"
"        with WaitForTopics([(\"turtle1/pose\", Pose)]) as waiter:\n"
"            assert waiter.topics_received() == {\"turtle1/pose\"}\n"
"            assert len(waiter.received_messages(\"turtle1/pose\")) >= 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:165
msgid ""
"This test creates a waiter that subscribes to the ``turtle1/pose`` topic "
"expecting ``Pose`` messages. The ``WaitForTopics`` class automatically "
"handles subscription and cleanup. The test asserts that the topic was "
"received and at least one message was captured."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:170
msgid "1.3.2 Topic subscription with triggered action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:172
msgid ""
"For more complex tests, you can trigger actions (such as publishing control "
"messages) using the ``trigger`` parameter. This allows you to verify that "
"nodes respond appropriately to a stimulus."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:175
msgid ""
"First, define a trigger function that will be called once publishers are "
"available:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:177
msgid ""
"def trigger_publish_twist(node: Node, linear_x: float, angular_z: float) -> "
"None:\n"
"    \"\"\"Publish Twist commands to trigger turtlesim pose updates.\n"
"\n"
"    Args:\n"
"        node: rclpy node used to create and reuse the cmd_vel publisher.\n"
"        linear_x: Linear x velocity in m/s\n"
"        angular_z: Angular z velocity in rad/s\n"
"\n"
"    Returns:\n"
"        None: Publishes messages as a side effect.\n"
"    \"\"\"\n"
"    matched_event = threading.Event()\n"
"\n"
"    def on_subscriber_matched(info: Any) -> None:\n"
"        if info.current_count > 0:\n"
"            matched_event.set()\n"
"\n"
"    if hasattr(node, \"cmd_vel_publisher\"):\n"
"        node.destroy_publisher(node.cmd_vel_publisher)\n"
"\n"
"    node.cmd_vel_publisher = node.create_publisher(\n"
"        Twist,\n"
"        \"turtle1/cmd_vel\",\n"
"        10,\n"
"        event_callbacks=PublisherEventCallbacks(matched=on_subscriber_matched),\n"
"    )\n"
"\n"
"    if not matched_event.wait(timeout=5.0):\n"
"        raise RuntimeError(\"Timed out waiting for turtlesim cmd_vel "
"subscriber\")\n"
"\n"
"    # Publish a Twist message to move the turtle\n"
"    msg = Twist()\n"
"    msg.linear.x = float(linear_x)\n"
"    msg.angular.z = float(angular_z)\n"
"    node.cmd_vel_publisher.publish(msg)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:215
msgid "Then use this trigger function in your test:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:217
msgid ""
"def test_moves_with_triggered_twist(self) -> None:\n"
"    \"\"\"Verify turtle motion after triggering Twist publication.\"\"\"\n"
"    waiter = WaitForTopics([(\"turtle1/pose\", Pose)], "
"trigger=trigger_publish_twist)\n"
"    try:\n"
"        while True:\n"
"            # Wait for the turtle to move by publishing a Twist message with "
"arguments\n"
"            # linear_x = 10 m/s and angular_z = 2*pi rad/s\n"
"            assert waiter.wait(linear_x=10, angular_z=2 * math.pi)\n"
"            assert waiter.topics_received() == {\"turtle1/pose\"}\n"
"            poses = waiter.received_messages(\"turtle1/pose\")\n"
"            assert len(poses) >= 1\n"
"            # Check that at least one of the received poses indicates motion "
"compatible with the\n"
"            # published Twist command\n"
"            if any(\n"
"                math.isclose(p.linear_velocity, 10.0, rel_tol=1e-2)\n"
"                and math.isclose(p.angular_velocity, 2 * math.pi, "
"rel_tol=1e-2)\n"
"                for p in poses\n"
"            ):\n"
"                break\n"
"    finally:\n"
"        waiter.shutdown()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:241
msgid "This test demonstrates:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:243
msgid "Creating a publisher and waiting for subscribers to be available"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:244
msgid ""
"Using ``WaitForTopics`` with a trigger function to publish messages and wait "
"for responses"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:245
msgid "Verifying that received messages match expected values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:247
msgid ""
"The while loop is necessary because the ``turtlesim`` node is constantly "
"publishing pose messages, and we want to wait until we receive a pose that "
"reflects the motion commanded by our published ``Twist`` message."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:250
msgid "1.4 Post-shutdown tests"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:252
msgid ""
"The classes marked with the ``launch_testing.post_shutdown_test`` decorator "
"are run after letting the nodes under test exit. A typical test here is "
"whether the nodes exited cleanly, for which ``launch_testing`` provides the "
"method `asserts.assertExitCodes <https://docs.ros.org/en/rolling/p/"
"launch_testing/launch_testing.asserts.html#launch_testing.asserts."
"assertExitCodes>`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:256
msgid ""
"# Post-shutdown tests\n"
"@launch_testing.post_shutdown_test()\n"
"class TestTurtleSimShutdown(unittest.TestCase):\n"
"    def test_exit_codes(self, proc_info):\n"
"        \"\"\"Check if the processes exited normally.\"\"\"\n"
"        launch_testing.asserts.assertExitCodes(proc_info)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:266
msgid "2 Register the test in the CMakeLists.txt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:268
msgid "Registering the test in the ``CMakeLists.txt`` fulfills two functions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:270
msgid ""
"it integrates it in the ``CTest`` framework ROS 2 CMake-based packages rely "
"on (and hence it will be called when running ``colcon test``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:272
msgid ""
"it allows to specify *how* the test is to be run - in this case, with a "
"unique domain id to ensure test isolation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:275
msgid ""
"This latter aspect is realized using the special test runner "
"`run_test_isolated.py <https://github.com/ros2/ament_cmake_ros/blob/rolling/"
"ament_cmake_ros/cmake/run_test_isolated.py>`_. To ease adding several "
"integration tests, we define the CMake function "
"``add_ros_isolated_launch_test`` such that each additional test requires "
"only a single line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/Developer-Tools/Debugging/Testing/Integration.rst:278
msgid ""
"cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.20)\n"
"project(app)\n"
"\n"
"########\n"
"# test #\n"
"########\n"
"\n"
"if(BUILD_TESTING)\n"
"  # Integration tests\n"
"  find_package(ament_cmake_ros REQUIRED)\n"
"  find_package(launch_testing_ament_cmake REQUIRED)\n"
"  function(add_ros_isolated_launch_test path)\n"
"    set(RUNNER \"${ament_cmake_ros_DIR}/run_test_isolated.py\")\n"
"    add_launch_test(\"${path}\" RUNNER \"${RUNNER}\" ${ARGN})\n"
"  endfunction()\n"
"  add_ros_isolated_launch_test(test/test_integration.py)\n"
"endif()"
msgstr ""

